        	PexAssert.AreEqual(Array.IndexOf(x, Old_index), Old_arrListIndexOfX >= 0 && Old_arrListIndexOfX >= Old_index);

            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" @@@  check x was not in list 
            PexAssert.IsTrue(Old_arrListContainsX == New_arrListContainsX && Old_arrListContainsX != x);
        
            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" @@@ check index is more than or equal to zero and less than or equal to list count 
            PexAssert.IsTrue(index >= 0 && index <= arrList.Count);

            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" @@@ check list count increased by one 
            PexAssert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount + 1, New_arrListCount);

            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" @@@ check index of x is more than or equal to inserted index 
            PexAssert.IsTrue(New_arrListIndexOfX == New_arrListLastIndexOfX && New_arrListIndexOfX >= Old_index);

            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" @@@ check x was added to the list and the index of x is 
            PexAssert.IsTrue(New_arrListContainsX == x && New_arrListIndexOfX == Old_index);
        }
    }
}

The full ArrayList implementation contract can be found here https://github.com/Microsoft/Pex/blob/master/Source/VisualStudio/Pex.VisualStudio.Plugin/CodeContracts/Collections/ArrayListContract.cs.


## <a name="BKMK_Limitations"></a>Limitations:

1. Code Contracts provide a very good static verification of your code for public-visible API's. They give you an idea about what could go wrong in your methods but it won’t help you write a good unit test.

2. Code Contracts are written in Microsoft's Visual Studio extensibility model, which means that the contracts are defined in the C# code. While this model works in a lot of cases, it doesn't translate well to unit testing frameworks.

3. Code Contracts can't catch all mistakes - they are best used as a way to catch simple mistakes and help write better code.

4. Code Contracts can be difficult to debug.

5. Contract can't verify a method or operation with a null value, can't prove that your code will not encounter an OutOfMemory Exception or divide by zero, etc.

6. Sometimes, the methods that are being invoked can violate the contract and that leads to a false positive verification.

7. Code Contracts can't catch all exceptions thrown by the code.

8. There is a performance impact. In some cases, creating contracts too fine can slow down the runtime of your program.

9. Code Contracts requires trust in the compiler to verify the contracts at all, this might not be realistic in enterprise codebases.

10. Code contracts won’t help when the method’s behavior depends non-deterministically on some uncontrollable external factors (like network services, for instance).

## <a name="BKMK_Conclusion"></a>Conclusion

Code Contracts are very useful in verifying the behavior of your application, especially when you have complex methods that may throw exceptions or return different results based on different input conditions. However, they are not a replacement for normal unit testing or other forms of software testing. It's an extra layer providing static error checking, but it does not ensure accurate testing in all cases or provide insights which you are not going to find out by normal unit testing.

Overall, when to use Code Contracts effectively is up to your team’s decision. If you’re building critical applications, it can be a very helpful tool to use at development time to provide insights about potential bugs and edge cases to ensure your code is safe and can handle more scenarios. But, if this additional responsibility is outweighed with potential performance and development time impact, it can be a disadvantage.

