1. Code Contracts (Microsoft Docs)
https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/modeling/code-contracts?view--------------------
            if ((Old_index <= Old_arrListIndexOfX && Old_index <= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX))
                Old_arrListIndexOfX++;
            else if (Old_index > Old_arrListIndexOfX && Old_index <= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX)
                Old_arrListLastIndexOfX++;

            // Invariant
            Invariant.IndexOutOfRange(index, 0, arrList.Count);
            
            // Contracts
            Contract.Requires(index >= 0);
            Contract.Requires(index <= arrList.Count);
            Contract.Ensures(arrList.Count.Equals(Old_arrListCount + 1));
            Contract.Ensures(arrList.IndexOf(x).Equals(Old_arrListIndexOfX + 1));
            Contract.Ensures(arrList.LastIndexOf(x).Equals(New_arrListLastIndexOfX + 1));

        }
    }
}

Here the "Invariant" and "Contract" are the conditions that you apply in your code, and PexObserve.ValueForViewing is used to record the values for viewing during fuzzing.

A: You may use PexMap (a dictionary equivalent from Pex) to simplify your testing.
For instance, instead of a generic ArrayList, you could use a PexMap<T> where T : new(), IComparable, IComparable<T>, IConvertible, IEquatable<T>, IFormattable, ISerializable where T : struct, class.
The PexMap class has a Insert method that you could leverage in your test method. 
public void PUT_InsertContract([PexAssumeUnderTest] PexMap<int> mapList, int x, int index)
{
...
}
However, Pex currently does not support PexMap. You may want to add this request to Microsoft's UserVoice page to help them prioritize it.
This way though, you still end up dealing with some complexity in your testing, you just don't have to worry about ArrayList-specific issues.
Regarding contracts, you should be able to use normal .Net Contracts. Here is a link to some of the basic usage, https://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/system.diagnostics.contracts.aspx.
In addition, you could use .Net Debug.Assert for your assertions, you can learn more on their usage here https://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/x53bbebb.aspx.
For view values, you could simply write them to console or debug via VS Output or Debugger display.
I hope this approach makes sense and provides a good guidance for your fuzzing tests.

A: Here is a better way to design your test using ArrayList and use the PexSettings(MaxNumberOfConditionsForMethodContracts = 50, MaxConditionsForPathBoundary = 50). Below is a simple example:

```

using System.Collections;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;
using Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.UnitTesting;

[PexClass(typeof(ArrayList))]
[PexMethodUnderTest("Add")]
public partial class ArrayListTest
{

    // Precondition
    [PexAssumeValid]
    public ArrayList list { get; private set; }

    [PexAssumeValid]
    public int index { get; private set; }

    [PexAssumeValid]
    public int value { get; private set; }

    // Precondition for array list
    [PexMethodUnderTest]
    [PexExplore]
    [HostType("Moles")]
    public void AddValueAtIndex()
    {
        //Arrange
        ArrayList arrayList = new ArrayList();

        //Act
        int originalCount = arrayList.Count;
        arrayList.Add(value);

        //Insert value at index
        arrayList.Insert(index, value);

        //Assert
        Assert.AreEqual(originalCount + 1, arrayList.Count);

        // Invariant
        Assert.IsTrue(index >= 0 && index <= arrayList.Count);

        //Postcondition
        Assert.AreEqual(value, arrayList[index]);

    }
}
```
This test is adding a value at a certain index in the ArrayList. The Pex setting(MaxNumberOfConditionsForMethod--------------------            if (Old_arrListIndexOfX == -1 || Old_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1)
            {
                Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX == Old_arrListIndexOfX + 1);
                Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
                Debug.Assert(New_arrListContainsX == Old_arrListContainsX);
            }

            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that the old array count is less than or equal to new array count
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount <= New_arrListCount);

            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ equality for contains method old array and new array 
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListContainsX == New_arrListContainsX);
        }
    }
}

//ArrayList.Test/ArrayListContractTest-Postcondition.cs
﻿using System.Collections;
using Pex.Framework;
using System.Diagnostics;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        [PexMethod(MaxExpectedException = INT32.MaxValue)]
        [PexPostConditions(nameof(POST_InsertContract))]
        public void PUT_InsertContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index <= arrList.Count && index >= 0);

            arrList.Insert(index, x);
        }

        [Conditional("Contracts")]
        public bool POST_InsertContract(ArrayList arrList, int x, int index, [PexAssumeValid]int Old_arrListCount)
        {
            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;

            // An assertion using Debug.Assert() that if inserted value was successfully added to the list, then new list count should be Old_arrListCount + 1
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount + 1);

            // If postcondition is false, then throw exception to fail the test
            return true; 
        }
    }
}

