It will add an element at specified index of existing array, checking if the new list contains this element and if its position is as expected.
Actually Pex doesn't add the constraints that the index is a valid index for the given ArrayList. So, you have to take care of valid insertion positions based on actual constraints.
For instance, it's not going to insert item at non-existent index (like Index = Count + 1). 
The PexInstrumenter doesn't check the count on insertion either, it only considers valid index positions between [0, Count]. 
I have created a branch in Github (at the time of writing this), it solves the issue and has additional Pex attributes for specifying the maximum Count value.
For this, clone the repo and build the solution.
If you encounter any other issues with my solution, please do let me know.

A: This is an interesting question. The default PexInstrumenter does not handle insertions in ArrayLists because the method in question is overloaded. The method with Index parameter accepts negative numbers as well as values larger than the list's count.
Here's how you can write a customized PexInstrumentationProvider. Over in Pex the contract for the Insert operation could look like this:

[PexMethod]
[PexCustomization("{Index:-1, Count}")]
public void Insert([PexAssumeNotNull]int[] items, int index, int item) { ... }

The attribute [PexCustomization("{Index:-1, Count}")] tells Pex to generate tests that include -1 and the value of Count when index is a member of the specified range. In essence, you enable Pex to generate a test for an insertion at an index == Count, indicating the new item should be added at the end without resizing the list.
If ArrayLists or linked lists handle insertions with a range of indices that goes from 0 to Count, this ought to be feasible. In case you have another approach, please share it so that I may update my answer. 

Please note: The above mentioned solution does not account for any possible edge cases like List being full and needing to resize which could complicate the Insert scenario. You may want to investigate different strategies to ensure your test coverage.

(Disclaimer: I am a developer at Microsoft)
Also, the solution for this problem is subject to change as the Pex Instrumenter may be designed to handle other cases in the future. So it might be good to follow the discussion and keep an eye on the Pex GitHub for future updates.
Q: How to remove the background of a button? I have a button that was styled using a PNG image, so the background color was changed using style.background. Now I need to remove the background image and just have the button display the text. I can accomplish this by removing the line style.background = 'image.png'. But to do this, wouldn't the button keep the same image or color as it was before? How do I just remove it?

A: The solution in question uses CSS. The button can be removed while maintaining the original function via CSS. Here is a simple solution:


button {
  background: none;
  color: #000000;
  border: none;
  cursor: pointer;
  text-decoration: underline;
}

button:hover {
  color: #ff0000;
}
<button id="myButton">My Button</button>


Now, the button won't have a background image. It'll just display the text as normal.--------------------            Debug.Assert((Old_arrListLastIndexOfX >= -1) && (Old_arrListLastIndexOfX <= Old_arrListCount - 1));

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ equals to old_arrList_count + 1
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount + 1);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ equals to index
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX == Old_index);
        }
    }
}
